# TBIT-style probe: SACK blocks arriving on a connection that DID
# NOT negotiate SACK_PERMITTED in the handshake MUST be ignored.
#
# RFC 2018 §2: SACK_PERMITTED is offered in the SYN / SYN-ACK and
# is symmetric — if either side omits it, the connection runs
# without SACK. A peer that later sends SACK blocks on such a
# connection is misbehaving (or hostile); receivers must not act on
# those SACK blocks (no enter_recovery, no selective retransmit).
#
# We verify: after a SYN-ACK without sackOK, an ACK carrying a SACK
# option does NOT trigger our RFC 6675 fast-retransmit. snd_una
# stays put, cwnd doesn't collapse, ssthresh is unchanged.

--connect 10.0.0.1:49152 10.0.0.2:80
+0 > SEW 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460, wscale 5, sackOK, TS val * ecr 0>

# SYN-ACK explicitly omits sackOK — SACK is now off for the lifetime
# of the connection.
+.005 < S. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 32768 <mss 1460, wscale 0, TS val 1 ecr *>
+0 > . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1>

# Send two MSS-payload-sized segments. Effective payload MSS with
# TS only = 1460 - 12 = 1448.
--send 2896
+0 > P. 1:1449(1448) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1>
+0 > P. 1449:2897(1448) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1>

# Peer sends an ACK at snd_una=1 carrying a SACK block as if the
# second segment had arrived (suggesting the first is missing).
# Even though our parser accepts the SACK option in the option
# table, our state machine must NOT act on it because SACK was
# never negotiated.
+.005 < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 32768 <sack 1449:2897, TS val 2 ecr *>

# No fast-retransmit should fire. Without SACK, the only fast-retx
# trigger is 3 dup-ACKs — we've only seen 1, so nothing emits.
# Confirm by sending one more byte of data (which DOES fire a normal
# data segment, not a retransmit). We have to advance the test by
# something observable; sending one more byte forces the stack to
# do *something* and lets us verify it didn't rewind to snd_una.
--send 1
# Note: any retx would land at seq=1; new data lands at snd_nxt=2897.
+0 > P. 2897:2898(1) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 2>
